The code statements that lead to the bug are:
for (i = 0; i < MLX5_MAX_PORTS; i++) {
    if (i == mlx5_get_dev_index(flow->priv->mdev))
        continue;
    mlx5e_tc_del_fdb_peer_flow(flow, i);
}
Reason:
The code iterates over all possible port indices up to `MLX5_MAX_PORTS`, assuming every index corresponds to a connected peer eswitch. When a peer device does not exist (e.g., in multi-port configurations with fewer ports), accessing the eswitch data structures for that index yields a NULL pointer, leading to the kernel NULL pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
devcom = flow->priv->mdev->priv.eswitch->devcom;
mlx5_devcom_for_each_peer_entry(devcom, peer_esw, pos) {
    i = mlx5_get_dev_index(peer_esw->dev);
    mlx5e_tc_del_fdb_peer_flow(flow, i);
}
Reason:
The fix uses `mlx5_devcom_for_each_peer_entry` to iterate only over actual connected peer devices via the devcom component. This ensures that only existing peers are processed, avoiding dereferences of non‑existent eswitch structures and preventing the NULL pointer crash.

